      "text": "To expand the product \\((2x+3)(x+5)\\), we will use the distributive property, which states that \\(a(b + c) = ab + ac\\). Here, we can distribute each term in the first binomial to each term in the second binomial.\n\nLet's start by distributing \\(2x\\) to both terms in the second binomial \\((2x+3)(x+5)\\):\n\n\\[2x \\cdot x + 2x \\cdot 5 = 2x^2 + 10x\\]\n\nNext, we will distribute \\(3\\) to both terms in the second binomial \\((2x+3)(x+5)\\):\n\n\\[3 \\cdot x + 3 \\cdot 5 = 3x + 15\\]\n\nNow, we combine all these results:\n\n\\[\n(2x+3)(x+5) = 2x^2 + 10x + 3x + 15\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\[\n2x^2 + (10x + 3x) + 15 = 2x^2 + 13x + 15\n\\]\n\nThus, the expanded form of \\((2x+3)(x+5)\\) is \\(\\boxed{2x^2 + 13x + 15}\\).\n\nLet's verify this using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, expand\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\nx = symbols('x')\r\n\r\n# Define the expression\r\nexpression = (2*x + 3)*(x + 5)\r\n\r\n# Expand the expression\r\nexpanded_expression = expand(expression)\r\nprint(expanded_expression)\n```",
